  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 909 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nCALM BEFORE THE STORM:\n\nA weak shortwave moved across the area today with only a few \nshowers and storms noted on radar. A few showers remain across\nthe area at this hour but are trending downwards and this will \ncontinue. Good news for the trick or treaters this evening. \nSatellite is showing plenty of cloud cover to our southwest, \npoised to move into the area very shortly. \n\nRAIN, SNOW, FLOODING POTENTIAL:\n\nMore widespread precipitation will begin falling between \nmidnight and 3am thanks to a potent Atmospheric River (AR). This\nAR remains forecast to bring anomalous precip to our CWA for \nthe next 24 to 36 hours. PWATs from the ensemble GFS and EC \nremain in the 250 - 350% range for this time of year, no change \nthere. Widespread rain and rainshowers, possibly some embedded \nthunderstorms, will bring valley rainfall to near/at an inch for\nthe central valleys, and 2 to 2.5 inches for the Four Corners \nvalleys eastward, less up north. 1.5 to 2.5 is likely for the \nBook Cliffs/Tavaputs and central mountains while the San Juans \nmay see 3 to 4 inches of liquid precip. The Flood Watch remains \nin effect for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado as already \nsaturated soils, along with this rainfall, may result in flash \nflooding and debris flow issues, not to mention river flooding. \nRivers are expected to respond quickly to this additional \nrainfall, especially in the Pagosa Springs area. \n\nAs far as snow is concerned, snow levels will remain above 10K\nfeet, if not a bit higher, before dropping Sunday afternoon\nonwards. Amounts remain in the 12 to 18 inch range with locally\nhigher amounts still possible so the Winter Storm Warning\nremains in effect. The Advisories also remain in effect for the\ncentral mountains, Grand Mesa, and Flat Tops where 5 to 10 \ninches are expected. As snow levels lower late Sunday night into\nMonday morning, some of this snow will reach pass level. Be \nprepared for sloppy driving conditions during the Monday \nmorning commute along US-550, US-50, and along I-70 over Vail \nPass. \n\nNEXT WEEK:\n\nBehind this system, conditions dry out for a few days as high \namplitude ridging moves overhead. We'll see a slight warm up through \nthe mid-week period, although highs remain 5-10 degrees below \nnormal for early November. Looking further out, a quick moving \nclipper system has the potential to bring some light showers to the \nregion mid-to-late next week, although model agreement is poor.",
